[StudyName "Chess Fundamentals, by J.R. Capablanca (Part 1 of 3)"]
[ChapterName "Ex.18: Openings"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/gfZY383j/iHVsXsLl"]
[Annotator "https://lichess.org/@/frankv53"]
[UTCDate "2021.02.16"]
[UTCTime "17:44:18"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 { A timid move, Black assumes a defensive attitude at once. On principle the move is wrong. In the openings, whenever possible, pieces should be moved in preference to Pawns. } 3. d4 { White takes the offensive immediately and strives to control the center } 3... Nd7 { Black does not wish to relinquish the center and also prefers the move to Nc6, but on principle the move is wrong because it blocks the action of the Queen's Bishop, and instead of facilitating the action of Black's pieces, it tends to cramp them. } 4. Bc4 h6 { Black is forced to pay the penalty of his previous move. Such a move on Black's part condemns by itself any form of opening that makes it necessary. } 5. Nc3 Ndf6 6. Be3 Be7 7. Qe2 { It should be noticed that White does not Castle yet. The reason is that he wants to deploy his forces first, and through the last move force Black to play } 7... c6 { to make room for the Queen as White threatens } 8. Rd1 { to be followed by dxe5. Black's other alternatives would finally force him to play ...exd4, thus abandoning the center to White. } 8... Qc7 9. O-O *


[Event "Chess Fundamentals, by J.R. Capablanca (Part 1 of 3): Ex.19: Openings"]
[Date "2021.02.16"]
[Result "*"]
