[ChapterName "Ex.23: A Classical Ending"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/gfZY383j/a5zwV7Ot"]
[FEN "8/6p1/7p/8/4k3/8/6KP/8 w - - 0 1"]
[SetUp "1"]
[UTCDate "2021.02.16"]
[UTCTime "17:44:18"]

{ The first part will be to get his King to h3, at the same time keeping intact the position of his Pawns. (This is all important, since, in order to win the game, it is essential at the end that Black may be able to advance his rearmost Pawn one or two squares according to the position of the White King.)
: }
1. Kg3 Ke3 2. Kg2 Kf4 3. Kf2 Kg4 4. Kg2 Kh4 5. Kg1 Kh3 { .
The first part has been completed.
The second part will be short and will consist in advancing the h-pawn up to the King. } 6. Kh1 h5 7. Kg1 h4 { .
This ends the second part.
The third part will consist in timing the advance of the g-pawn so as to play ...g6 when the White King is at h1. It now becomes evident how necessary it is to be able to move the g-pawn either one or two squares according to the position of the White King, as indicated previously. } 8. Kh1 { In this case, as White is in the corner, the Pawn will be advanced two squares. If the White King were at g1 the g-pawn should only be advanced one square. } 8... g5 9. Kg1 g4 10. Kh1 g3 11. hxg3 (11. Kg1 g2) 11... hxg3 12. Kg1 g2 13. Kf2 Kh2 { .
It is in this analytical way that the student should try to learn. He will thus train his mind to follow a logical sequence in reasoning out any position. This example is excellent training, since it is easy to divide it into three stages and to explain the main point of each part. } *


