{ On the other hand, if: }
1. Kh1! (1. Kf1? Kd2 2. Kf2 Kd3) 1... Kd2 (1... g4 { White does not play 2 fxg4, because 2...e4 will win, but plays: } 2. Kg2 Kd2 (2... gxf3+ 3. Kxf3 { , followed by 4 Ke4 , will draw. }) 3. fxg4 e4 { and mere counting will show that both sides Queen, drawing the game. }) 2. Kh2 Kd3 3. Kh3! Ke2 4. Kg2 Ke3 5. Kg3 Kd4 6. Kg4 { attacking the Pawn and forcing Black to play 6...Ke3 when he can go back to g3 as already shown, and always keep the opposition. } *


